WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.610
An array is the fastest container there is.

2
00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.670
Ask for item number five hundred thousand

3
00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.910
and the computer goes straight to it

4
00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.900
because the position is the key.

5
00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:16.660
Now suppose the keys are not small numbers.

6
00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.600
They are names, or words, or order numbers.

7
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.370
Can we keep that speed?

8
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.370
The idea: turn the key into a position.

9
00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.079
Do the arithmetic once

10
00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.871
and jump straight to the slot

11
00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:31.690
where that key belongs.

12
00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:33.680
That is hashing.

13
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.917
This chapter is about maps that

14
00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.330
find a key in about one step

15
00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:40.420
without keeping the keys in order.

16
00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.510
In this video we'll see how a hash function

17
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.390
turns a key into a number

18
00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.018
how that number becomes a

19
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.360
position in the table

20
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:52.250
and what makes a hash function good, or bad.

21
00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:56.070
A hash function works in two steps.

22
00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.450
First, the hash code: any key, a word

23
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:01.930
a pair, a record

24
00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:04.860
becomes an ordinary whole number.

25
00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:07.980
Second, compression: that number

26
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.412
which can be huge or even negative

27
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.474
is squeezed into a table index

28
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.170
between zero and m minus one.

29
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.190
The simplest compression is the remainder

30
00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:21.795
the index is the hash code, modulo m.

31
00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:25.030
If the table has thirteen slots and the

32
00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.650
hash code is ninety-nine million

33
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.490
one hundred sixty-two thousand

34
00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:30.810
three hundred twenty-two

35
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.480
the index is twelve.

36
00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.412
Choosing m to be a prime number helps

37
00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.487
because a prime does not share factors

38
00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.550
with the patterns in real keys.

39
00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:41.311
There is a catch.

40
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.309
There are far more possible

41
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.140
keys than table slots

42
00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.205
so two different keys will sometimes

43
00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:48.560
land in the same slot.

44
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.650
This is called a collision.

45
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.450
Collisions cannot be avoided.

46
00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.650
We can only hope to make them rare

47
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.590
and handle them when they happen.

48
00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:00.160
The next two videos are about handling them.

49
00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.826
Here is a collision you can check yourself.

50
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.917
The words "A a" and "B B"

51
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.560
are different keys

52
00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:09.340
but their hash codes are both two thousand

53
00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:10.470
one hundred twelve.

54
00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.620
What makes a hash function good?

55
00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.629
Mostly, one thing: it must spread real keys

56
00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.770
evenly over the table.

57
00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.530
Here is a tempting hash function

58
00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.480
add up the character codes.

59
00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:27.205
Let's test it on the seven hundred twenty

60
00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.280
ways to rearrange the letters a to f.

61
00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.710
Rearranging letters never changes their sum.

62
00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.282
So all seven hundred twenty keys

63
00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:37.960
get the same hash code

64
00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.639
and fall into one single bucket.

65
00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.051
A table meant to take one step now

66
00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.590
takes seven hundred twenty.

67
00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.304
Now a hash function that cares about

68
00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.030
the position of each letter

69
00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:50.802
called a polynomial hash.

70
00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.018
The same seven hundred twenty keys get seven

71
00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.030
hundred twenty different hash codes

72
00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.940
and the longest bucket holds only four.

73
00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.160
Same keys, same table.

74
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.770
The only difference is the hash function.

75
00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.026
The polynomial hash works like this.

76
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.764
Treat the characters as digits of a

77
00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.520
number written in base thirty-one

78
00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.910
so the first letter counts most.

79
00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.379
The hash code of a word is its

80
00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:18.331
first character times thirty-one

81
00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:19.986
to the power n minus one

82
00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.048
plus the second times thirty-one

83
00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.350
to the n minus two, and so on

84
00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:25.780
down to the last character.

85
00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.028
Java uses exactly this rule for strings

86
00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.941
and the arithmetic wraps around

87
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.100
at thirty-two bits

88
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.440
so some hash codes come out negative.

89
00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.020
That is fine

90
00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:37.890
as long as we compress them correctly.

91
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.413
For example "stop" and "pots"

92
00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:42.910
have the same letters.

93
00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:44.490
Their sums are equal

94
00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.350
four hundred fifty-four.

95
00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:49.950
Their polynomial hash codes are not

96
00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:51.890
three million five hundred forty thousand

97
00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:53.566
nine hundred ninety-four

98
00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:55.256
and three million four hundred

99
00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:56.750
forty-six thousand

100
00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:58.000
nine hundred seventy-four.

101
00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:01.318
There are two rules a key must follow

102
00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:03.265
and breaking either one makes

103
00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.060
the map quietly lose data.

104
00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:09.839
Rule one: keys that are equal must

105
00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:11.300
have equal hash codes.

106
00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.294
If two equal keys hashed to different slots

107
00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:15.794
we would look in the wrong slot

108
00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.810
and never find what we stored.

109
00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.227
Rule two: a key must not change while

110
00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:22.830
it is in the table.

111
00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:25.530
Its hash code is used once, when it goes in.

112
00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:27.590
If the key changes afterwards

113
00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:30.070
it sits in the slot of its old value

114
00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:31.210
and a lookup

115
00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:33.830
with either the old value or the new one

116
00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.160
looks somewhere else.

117
00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:37.900
The entry is still there

118
00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.060
counted in the size.

119
00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.950
It is just unreachable.

120
00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:42.630
To sum up.

121
00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:43.948
A hash function turns a

122
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.508
key into a hash code

123
00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.217
and compression turns that

124
00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:48.770
into a table index.

125
00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.870
Collisions are unavoidable

126
00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:54.308
so what matters is spreading keys evenly

127
00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.669
add up the letters and every

128
00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:58.050
anagram collides

129
00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:01.040
weigh the positions and they spread.

130
00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:04.480
And two rules for keys: equal keys

131
00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.120
equal hash codes

132
00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:09.510
and never change a key inside a table.

133
00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:10.890
Next time

134
00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:13.190
we handle collisions the simplest way

135
00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:15.380
let every slot hold a chain.

136
00:05:16.007 --> 00:05:18.407
Now the same ideas in Java.

137
00:05:18.987 --> 00:05:22.817
Here is the polynomial hash in Java.

138
00:05:22.817 --> 00:05:23.997
The same loop

139
00:05:23.997 --> 00:05:26.537
multiply the running value by the base

140
00:05:26.537 --> 00:05:28.147
add the next character.

141
00:05:28.497 --> 00:05:30.655
An int in Java wraps around at

142
00:05:30.727 --> 00:05:32.227
thirty-two bits by itself

143
00:05:32.227 --> 00:05:33.667
so no helper is needed.

144
00:05:33.727 --> 00:05:36.432
With base thirty-one this is exactly

145
00:05:36.507 --> 00:05:38.107
what the String class computes.

146
00:05:38.107 --> 00:05:41.287
If you call hash code on the string hello

147
00:05:41.287 --> 00:05:43.507
you get ninety-nine million

148
00:05:43.507 --> 00:05:45.447
one hundred sixty-two thousand

149
00:05:45.447 --> 00:05:46.777
three hundred twenty-two.

150
00:05:47.127 --> 00:05:49.258
Java also caches that value

151
00:05:49.337 --> 00:05:50.436
inside the string

152
00:05:50.497 --> 00:05:51.984
so hashing a string a second

153
00:05:52.037 --> 00:05:53.527
time costs nothing.

154
00:05:54.107 --> 00:05:56.287
Compression has a trap in Java.

155
00:05:56.357 --> 00:05:58.447
The percent operator keeps the

156
00:05:58.517 --> 00:05:59.757
sign of the left side

157
00:05:59.757 --> 00:06:02.657
so minus three percent seven is minus three.

158
00:06:02.657 --> 00:06:05.227
A negative index would crash the table.

159
00:06:05.577 --> 00:06:07.687
The fix is floor mod

160
00:06:07.787 --> 00:06:09.324
which always returns a value between

161
00:06:09.367 --> 00:06:11.567
zero and m minus one

162
00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:14.297
for minus three and seven, it gives four.

163
00:06:14.647 --> 00:06:16.896
And because integer overflow produces

164
00:06:16.957 --> 00:06:19.357
negative hash codes all the time

165
00:06:19.357 --> 00:06:21.047
this is not a rare case.

166
00:06:21.397 --> 00:06:23.928
The demo prints hello in thirteen

167
00:06:24.007 --> 00:06:26.111
slots at index twelve

168
00:06:26.207 --> 00:06:28.389
the two strings "A a" and "B

169
00:06:28.467 --> 00:06:30.131
B" with the same code

170
00:06:30.207 --> 00:06:32.083
and the sums and polynomial

171
00:06:32.147 --> 00:06:35.037
codes of stop and pots.

172
00:06:35.617 --> 00:06:37.866
Java states the rules for keys in the

173
00:06:37.927 --> 00:06:40.187
contract of equals and hash code.

174
00:06:40.187 --> 00:06:42.087
If two objects are equal

175
00:06:42.087 --> 00:06:44.257
they must return the same hash code.

176
00:06:44.607 --> 00:06:46.451
This class compares readings

177
00:06:46.517 --> 00:06:47.907
by temperature only

178
00:06:47.977 --> 00:06:50.082
but mixes the reading number

179
00:06:50.157 --> 00:06:51.517
into the hash code.

180
00:06:51.517 --> 00:06:54.417
Two equal readings, put in a hash map

181
00:06:54.417 --> 00:06:56.727
the second one is not found.

182
00:06:57.077 --> 00:06:58.683
The mutable point behaves

183
00:06:58.747 --> 00:07:00.087
like the Python one.

184
00:07:00.087 --> 00:07:02.151
We store it, change its x

185
00:07:02.227 --> 00:07:04.129
and neither the old value nor the

186
00:07:04.187 --> 00:07:05.687
new value finds the entry

187
00:07:05.687 --> 00:07:07.957
though the map still counts it.

188
00:07:08.307 --> 00:07:10.777
And if a class overrides equals

189
00:07:10.857 --> 00:07:12.770
but forgets hash code

190
00:07:12.857 --> 00:07:14.713
it inherits the identity hash

191
00:07:14.777 --> 00:07:16.127
code from the object class

192
00:07:16.177 --> 00:07:18.327
so two equal objects get

193
00:07:18.417 --> 00:07:19.497
different hash codes

194
00:07:19.497 --> 00:07:21.643
and the lookup returns null.

195
00:07:21.717 --> 00:07:23.198
Whenever you override one

196
00:07:23.257 --> 00:07:25.587
of the two methods, override both.

197
00:07:26.167 --> 00:07:28.461
Tests pin all this down.

198
00:07:28.557 --> 00:07:31.551
The polynomial hash with base thirty-one must

199
00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:34.217
equal the string class's own hash code

200
00:07:34.217 --> 00:07:36.677
for hello, for the empty string

201
00:07:36.677 --> 00:07:38.687
and for a very long word.

202
00:07:39.037 --> 00:07:42.627
One famous word, polygenelubricants

203
00:07:42.727 --> 00:07:45.027
has a hash code that is exactly

204
00:07:45.127 --> 00:07:48.767
the most negative int: minus two billion

205
00:07:48.767 --> 00:07:50.787
one hundred forty-seven million

206
00:07:50.787 --> 00:07:52.567
four hundred eighty-three thousand

207
00:07:52.567 --> 00:07:53.807
six hundred forty-eight.

208
00:07:53.807 --> 00:07:56.535
Its absolute value is still negative

209
00:07:56.607 --> 00:07:58.169
which is one more reason to

210
00:07:58.227 --> 00:07:59.757
compress with floor mod.

211
00:08:00.337 --> 00:08:01.881
Mistakes to avoid.

212
00:08:01.967 --> 00:08:03.989
Using the percent operator

213
00:08:04.067 --> 00:08:06.007
instead of floor mod.

214
00:08:06.007 --> 00:08:09.407
Overriding equals and not hash code.

215
00:08:09.407 --> 00:08:12.227
Hashing a field that equals ignores.

216
00:08:12.227 --> 00:08:15.057
And changing a key inside a table.

217
00:08:15.407 --> 00:08:17.797
One more bites in tests

218
00:08:17.897 --> 00:08:19.380
comparing string keys with

219
00:08:19.437 --> 00:08:20.951
the double equals sign.

220
00:08:21.017 --> 00:08:23.586
Two equal strings built at different

221
00:08:23.657 --> 00:08:25.549
times are different objects

222
00:08:25.617 --> 00:08:27.518
and a map that compared identity

223
00:08:27.577 --> 00:08:29.237
would not find them.

224
00:08:29.237 --> 00:08:31.967
Always compare keys with equals.

225
00:08:32.547 --> 00:08:35.437
To recap: polynomial hashing

226
00:08:35.437 --> 00:08:37.957
floor mod for compression

227
00:08:37.957 --> 00:08:39.657
equal keys hash equally

228
00:08:39.657 --> 00:08:42.587
and never change a key inside a table.

229
00:08:42.937 --> 00:08:45.057
Next, the chains.

